# Bytes a rule of Catalan's names by address, which no lifter writes: these
# are ours. The phonemes are the phone statement's own alphabet, which
# tools/lang-phonemes.py caes prints.
#
# Catalan has two affricates Castilian has not. The voiced postalveolar --
# metge, platja, viatge -- and the voiced alveolar -- dotze, setze. Spanish
# declares a code for the first, J, and never reaches it from its own
# spelling, so nothing in the module speaks it: there is no span_ph_J, and no
# arm of the value chain that would expand it the way C is expanded into a
# stop and a fricative. Inserting it laid down a phone nothing could say and
# the word stopped where it stood.
#
# So a letter rule lays down the two phones itself, which is the same two
# sounds in the same order and asks nothing of the module that is not already
# there. That is what Polish did with its own hard affricates for the same
# reason.
bytes cat_ph_dzz 05 12   # d, then the voiced postalveolar
bytes cat_ph_dz  05 0e   # d, then a voiced s

# The schwa, which is code 37 and the module's first phoneme of its own.
# Central Catalan reduces every unstressed a and e to it, and it is the sound
# that makes the language recognisable: casa is not [kasa] but [kaza], and
# the last vowel is neither a nor e.
bytes cat_ph_schwa 25

# The two open vowels, and the two letters that write them.
#
# Catalan has seven stressed vowels where Castilian has five, and the pairs it
# adds are the open e and the open o: sec and sec, dona and dona, are one
# letter apart in writing and one vowel apart in speech. The accent is what
# tells them apart -- the acute is the close vowel and the grave the open one
# -- so the grave letters have to reach a rule of their own rather than being
# flattened to a plain vowel on the way in.
bytes cat_letter_egrave 62   # e with a grave, by its code in the alphabet
bytes cat_letter_ograve 64   # o with a grave
bytes cat_ph_eopen 26        # the open e, phone 38
bytes cat_ph_oopen 27        # the open o, phone 39

# The digits, as Catalan says them. They are characters rather than phonemes:
# a number word goes in as letters and the letter rules then read it, which is
# the same path a letter's own name takes. tools/lang-alphabet.py caes says
# which code is which character.
#
# Castilian says these through span_char_name, a dictionary, and changing a
# word there wants an action whose record is a length that rule already lays
# down -- and even a rewrite that keeps the length comes out silent, because
# what delta-dict.py writes is reached by an address the arena does not know.
# So the digits are laid down by a rule instead, out of bytes of ours, which
# is a store the arena is told about.
#
# Most are spelled with an accent, and the accent does two jobs. A vowel
# that carries one takes the word's stress, which is what puts vintiun's
# stress on the un and vintissis's on the sis, where the stress rule, reading
# the last letter, put both a syllable back. And the accent says which of
# the two mid vowels it is: set is [s E t] and nou [n O w], open, so they
# carry a grave, and dos is [d o s], close, so it carries an acute. On its
# own each digit is one syllable and the accent changes nothing but the
# vowel; in a compound it is the stress.
#
# Two are left plain, because their accented spelling reads wrong here and
# the stress rule already puts them right: quatre ends in a vowel and takes
# the penultimate, cinc ends in a consonant and takes the last, alone and
# after vinti alike. An a with a grave is dropped by the letter rules, so
# quatre with one came out [k w t r e], and a c before an i with an acute is
# read as a k, so cinc with one came out [k i ng k].
#
# un is written hun, because an h says nothing and an i before a u would
# otherwise make the u a glide: vintihun is [b i n t i u n] and not
# [b i n t i w n]. sis and set are written with two esses, because a single s
# between two vowels is voiced in Catalan and here it must not be: vintissis
# is [b i n t i s i s].
bytes cat_num_zero    1f 02 18 04         # zero
bytes cat_num_un      10 6f 15            # hun, u acute
bytes cat_num_dos     0d 6e 19            # dos, o acute
bytes cat_num_tres    1a 18 62 19         # tres, e grave
bytes cat_num_quatre  17 05 01 1a 18 02   # quatre
bytes cat_num_cinc    0c 03 15 0c         # cinc
bytes cat_num_sis     19 19 6d 19         # ssis, i acute
bytes cat_num_set     19 19 62 1a         # sset, e grave
bytes cat_num_vuit    1b 6f 03 1a         # vuit, u acute
bytes cat_num_nou     15 64 05            # nou, o grave

